title: Pseudohypoparathyroidism
---

# Pseudohypoparathyroidism

- **Definition** - inherited disorder characterised by tissue resistance to the effects of PTH, such that PTH levels are significantly elevated although the individual is functionally hypothyroid
- **Pathophysiology of pseudohypoparathyroidism:**
  - <u>Mutation of GNAS1</u> - nactivation of Gs-alpha protein involved in downstream signal transduction of the PTH receptor and other GPCRs, resulting in tissue resistance to effects of PTH
  - <u>Biochemical manifestations</u> - only occurs in Type 1a pseudohypoparathyroidism when the GNAS mutation is inherited on maternal chromosome (example of genetic imprinting as renal cells only express maternal GNAS1)
  - <u>Additional syndromal features</u> - see below
- **Subtypes of pseudohypoparathyroidism** - classified by biochemical and syndromal features:
  - <u>Type 1a pseudohypoparathyroidism</u> - evidence of hypocalcaemia, hyperphosphataemia and other skeletal manifestations (APO) only if allele is from the materal chromosome
  - <u>Type 2 pseudohypoparathyroidism</u> - unknown mechanism downstream of the signally pathway resulting in biochemical manifestation in the absence of APO
- **Clinical features of type 1a pseudohypoparathyroidism:**
  - <u>Hypoparathyroidism</u> - hypoCa, hyperPO4 despite high PTH
  - <u>Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy</u>:
    - Short stature
    - Short fourth metacarpals andn metatarsals
    - Round face
    - Obesity
    - Subcutaneous calcification
- **Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism** - presents w/ clinical features of AHO but biochemically normal, caused by by inheritence of the paternal GNAS1 allele (expressed in skeletal tissues but not renal tissues)
